---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import AutoRelated from '../../components/AutoRelated.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';

const article = {
  publishDate: '2026-04-20',
  modifiedDate: '2026-07-21',
  section: 'Policy & Legislation',
};
const topics = ['policy', 'legislation', 'psychedelics'];
const tags = ['policy-watch'];

const faqItems = [
  {
    question: 'Is psilocybin legal in Maine?',
    answer: 'No. Maine law lists psilocybin and psilocyn as Schedule X drugs. Unlawful possession of a Schedule X drug is a Class D crime. LD 1034 did not become law, and no later federal action changed Maine law.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Did LD 1034 create a Maine psilocybin commission or program?',
    answer: 'No. The Maine Legislature records the final disposition of LD 1034 as “Enactment Failed” on June 10, 2025. It created no commission, regulated services program, report deadline, or implementation timeline.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Did the April 2026 executive order approve or legalize psilocybin?',
    answer: 'No. Executive Order 14401 directs federal agencies to prioritize certain research, access, review, and possible product-specific rescheduling work. It does not approve a psilocybin product, legalize possession, or change Maine law.',
  },
];

const faqPageSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.question,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.answer },
  })),
};
---

<Layout
  title="Is Psilocybin Legal in Maine in 2026? LD 1034 Status"
  description="Psilocybin remains illegal in Maine. LD 1034 failed enactment, created no commission or program, and federal research action did not legalize or approve psilocybin."
  heroImage="/images/heroes/maine-psilocybin-2026.jpg"
  article={article}
  topics={topics}
  tags={tags}
>
  <style>
    article { max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto; }
    .article-header { margin-bottom: 2rem; padding-bottom: 1.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
    .subtitle { font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--color-text-light); }
    section { margin-bottom: 2.5rem; }
    h2 { margin-top: 0; color: var(--color-primary); }
    ul { padding-left: 1.5rem; }
    li { margin-bottom: 0.65rem; }
    .sources, .disclaimer { padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem; border-radius: 0.75rem; }
    .sources { background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-soft-green) 6%, transparent); }
    .disclaimer { background: var(--color-disclaimer-bg); border: 1px solid var(--color-disclaimer-border); color: var(--color-disclaimer-text); font-size: 0.9rem; }
    .review-note { font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--color-text-light); border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border); padding-top: 1.25rem; }
  </style>

  <article>
    <header class="article-header">
      <h1>Is psilocybin legal in Maine in 2026?</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">No. LD 1034 failed enactment, and Maine has no psilocybin commission, services program, or implementation timeline.</p>
    </header>

    <Callout title="Current Maine status" type="warning">
      Maine lists psilocybin and psilocyn as Schedule X drugs. Unlawful possession of a Schedule X drug is a Class D crime. A Class D sentence can include less than one year of imprisonment and a fine of up to $2,000, although an actual sentence depends on the case.
    </Callout>

    <section>
      <h2>LD 1034 did not become law</h2>
      <p><a href="https://legislature.maine.gov/legis/bills/display_ps.asp?LD=1034&snum=132" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Legislature's official bill page</a> records the final disposition of LD 1034 as <strong>“Enactment Failed” on June 10, 2025</strong>. The proposal would have changed the treatment of certain personal possession under Maine law, but the failed bill changed nothing.</p>
      <p>LD 1034 did not create a study commission or advisory board. Maine therefore has no commission report deadline, regulated psilocybin-services program, licensing process, launch date, or implementation schedule to track from this bill.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What Maine law says now</h2>
      <p><a href="https://legislature.maine.gov/statutes/17-A/title17-Asec1102.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Title 17-A, §1102(2)(C)</a> lists psilocybin and psilocyn in Schedule X. <a href="https://legislature.maine.gov/statutes/17-A/title17-Asec1107-A.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Section 1107-A(1)(D)</a> classifies unlawful possession of a Schedule X drug as a Class D crime.</p>
      <p>The general Class D limits are <a href="https://legislature.maine.gov/statutes/17-A/title17-Asec1604.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">less than one year of imprisonment</a> and a <a href="https://legislature.maine.gov/statutes/17-A/title17-Asec1704.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">maximum fine of $2,000</a>. These are maximum authorized penalties, not a prediction of the outcome in any individual case. For a plain-language overview of how Maine treats psychedelic mushrooms versus cannabis, see <a href="/blog/are-mushrooms-legal-in-maine">Are Mushrooms Legal in Maine?</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Federal action is not approval or legalization</h2>
      <p><a href="https://www.whitehouse.gov/presidential-actions/2026/04/accelerating-medical-treatments-for-serious-mental-illness/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Executive Order 14401</a>, signed April 18, 2026, directs federal agencies to support psychedelic research, certain Right to Try pathways, priority review for qualifying products, and possible product-specific rescheduling after Phase 3 trials and FDA approval.</p>
      <p>The order does not itself approve a psilocybin product, reschedule psilocybin generally, authorize retail sales, or override Maine criminal law. The <a href="https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/07/14/2026-14155/considerations-for-potential-future-therapeutic-use-of-psychedelic-drugs-public-hearing-request-for" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FDA's July 2026 public-hearing notice</a> concerns potential future therapeutic use and data standards. A hearing or expedited review is not a drug approval.</p>
      <p>The <a href="https://www.dea.gov/factsheets/psilocybin" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">DEA continues to identify psilocybin as a controlled hallucinogen</a>. Federal research developments do not provide a legal path for Maine cannabis licensees to possess, manufacture, advertise, or sell psilocybin.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What Maine cannabis operators should do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Keep psilocybin outside licensed cannabis premises, inventory, marketing, events, and staff activity.</li>
        <li>Do not describe a study, hearing, voucher, or executive order as FDA approval or legalization.</li>
        <li>Check enacted Maine law and final federal agency action before changing policy. Bill introductions and forecasts are not operative law.</li>
        <li>Use qualified legal counsel for a fact-specific compliance question.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>For current cannabis requirements, use the <a href="/guides/maine-cannabis-regulations">Maine cannabis regulations guide</a> and OCP's official rules rather than treating psychedelic policy as an extension of the cannabis program.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <Faq faqs={faqItems} withoutSchema={true} />
    </section>

    <section class="sources">
      <h2>Primary sources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://legislature.maine.gov/legis/bills/display_ps.asp?LD=1034&snum=132" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Maine Legislature: LD 1034 text and final disposition</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://legislature.maine.gov/statutes/17-A/title17-Asec1102.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">17-A M.R.S. §1102: drug schedules</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://legislature.maine.gov/statutes/17-A/title17-Asec1107-A.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">17-A M.R.S. §1107-A: unlawful possession</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.whitehouse.gov/presidential-actions/2026/04/accelerating-medical-treatments-for-serious-mental-illness/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">White House: Executive Order 14401</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/07/14/2026-14155/considerations-for-potential-future-therapeutic-use-of-psychedelic-drugs-public-hearing-request-for" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FDA notice, 91 FR 43095</a></li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="disclaimer">
      <p><strong>Legal-information notice:</strong> This page summarizes public sources and is not legal or medical advice. Laws, court outcomes, and agency actions can change.</p>
    </section>

    <p class="review-note">Editorially reviewed against the cited primary sources by <strong>Maine Dispensary Guide</strong> on <strong>2026-07-21</strong>. This is organizational editorial review, not legal, medical, tax, or other professional review. See the <a href="/about/corrections">editorial corrections log</a>.</p>

    <AutoRelated currentPath="/blog/maine-psilocybin-2026-guide" currentTopics={topics} section="Policy & Legislation" limit={6} />
  </article>

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqPageSchema)}></script>
</Layout>
